{"kind":"propose","text":"CUT-R first act, source only. The six findings below support preparation after CUT-A. Two points need your decision before code: the repository has no unsigned pack command, and a final release digest needs the publisher's signature. I propose one final reviewed head after you return the public signed packets. Before that head, a reproducible preparation command can produce unsigned signing drafts without opening a key.\n\n1. Search of the old Grow release prefix, publisher ID and DEMO_RELEASE/DEMO_PUBLISHER found these 23 files. The search included hidden source and documentation files, and excluded Git, custody directories, dependencies and build outputs:\n- apps/room/wrangler.grow.jsonc\n- apps/room/wrangler.demo.jsonc\n- apps/room/wrangler.connect.jsonc\n- apps/room/worker.grow.ts\n- apps/room/page/assets.ts\n- examples/grow/demo/application.dap.json\n- examples/grow/demo-release.ts\n- examples/grow/runbook.md\n- examples/grow/simulator-worker.ts\n- examples/grow/simulator-host.ts\n- examples/grow/simulator-release.test.ts\n- packages/platform/src/application-codec/application-package-v2.ts\n- packages/platform/test/package-graph.test.ts\n- packages/scope/test/support/recorded-grow-release.ts\n- packages/scope/test/support/maintained-applications-child.ts\n- notes/implementation/2026-10-19-g5-hosted-demo.md\n- notes/implementation/2026-10-19-p1-package-validator.md\n- notes/implementation/2026-10-20-b1-member-elements.md\n- notes/implementation/2026-10-22-security-union.md\n- notes/implementation/2026-10-30-docs-relocated-text.md\n- notes/implementation/2026-11-03-o4-connect.md\n- notes/implementation/2026-11-10-o4-connect-runbook-record.md\n- notes/implementation/cd-dropped-kind-checks.md\nHistorical notes retain their facts. Generated Page assets need their normal generator if a maintained input changes. Definition pins also occur in apps/room/archive-policy.ts and examples/grow/simulator-host.ts. Indirect recorded-packet consumers include apps/room/lanes/test/grow-archive.test.ts, packages/scope/test/archive-installed.test.ts, packages/scope/test/archive-publication.test.ts, packages/scope/test/support/archive-native-authority.ts and packages/derive/test/definition-corpus/shipped.ts. Each consumer must select either the retained old packet or the new packet deliberately.\n\n2. Keep EARLIER_RELEASE and EARLIER_PUBLISHER fixed in application-package-v2.ts:30–40. They admit only the earlier nine-key semantic shape and its exact signed release. New pack input still requires full composition (lines 152–169). Old archives need their original semantic bytes and signed release proof to verify the recorded install under its original platform rules. application-install-v2.ts:29–42 calls readPublishedApplicationReleaseProof2 on those retained values. Changing the exception to the new release would refuse the old proof. Preserve the old signed packet as an immutable fixture, with separate old public constants, before replacing the current demo packet. package-graph.test.ts:49–61 currently reads the demo path and DEMO constants while asserting the old shape. That regression must continue to exercise the old bytes after CUT-R.\n\n3. Existing packet commands, shown but not run, are:\n  node --import tsx examples/grow/prepare-demo.ts /tmp/cut-r-grow-source-new\n  node packages/cli/bin/dap.js pack /tmp/cut-r-grow-source-new --publisher \u003cGrow-key-name-from-Hugh\u003e --out /tmp/cut-r-grow-signed-new.dap.json\n  node packages/cli/bin/dap.js pack . --config examples/lending/dap.application.json --publisher release --out /tmp/cut-r-lending-signed-new.dap.json\nRun them from the repository root with fresh output paths. Grow's preparation derives demo definitions from maintained room.ts and site.ts through demoDefinitions(), copies check presentation sources and adds demo/simulator.json (prepare-demo.ts and prepare-check.ts). Lending's canonical source is definition.ts, whose scripts/pin.mjs writes definition.json and pin.ts. dap.application.json binds those definitions and the browser sources. The Lending command is recorded in examples/lending/APPLICATION.md:3–14.\n\nThese commands sign. packCommand accepts only config, out and publisher, loads an existing named signer and calls packDirectory (packages/cli/src/application-command.ts:80–103). There is no --unsigned flag. packApplication2 creates the manifest, calls signer.sign, then reads the signed packet (application-package-v2.ts:152–169). Its release ID hashes the release object including sig (lines 23,102–109). A draft cannot supply the final DEMO_RELEASE without that signature. A public TEST signature would produce a different release and must not stand for a production unsigned packet.\n\nProposed preparation command for your acceptance, not an existing command: node --import tsx scripts/prepare-cutover-releases.ts --out \u003cnew-directory\u003e. Extract canonical source capture and unsigned v2 preparation from the existing pack path. Both the draft command and later signing use those same functions. The command writes canonical semantic and presentation bytes, their IDs, the public publisher manifest and exact signing bytes. It writes no signature or final release ID. Repeated preparation must reproduce each draft byte for byte. This needs only public publisher identities. Then you sign outside the checkout. Builder imports the returned public packets, sets final constants, runs the changed witnesses and gate once, and submits one final head. Accept this timing, or choose an unsigned-draft head followed by a separate final-pins head. The requested final constants and production-unsigned packet cannot coexist truthfully in one completed release head with the current wire format.\n\n4. Until AE-1, GROW_ARCHIVE_CONFIG.release keeps the old sha256:71e10ca9499e926a4de1d8294ed83a772051dff677d8b1687bbd8e28e61802fa, with its old directory and repository. Do not change it to the new DEMO_RELEASE in CUT-R. The exact comparison in apps/room/archive.ts:26–34 returns null on a release mismatch. worker.grow.ts:19–25 then returns the base wiring before it reads the signed packet or constructs policies. Base wiring has no archive owner (packages/scope/src/worker.ts:798–815). object-archive.ts:12–22 therefore creates no archive driver. This mismatch disables Grow archive wiring without a throw or archive retry.\n\n5. Signing key names only: Lending uses the existing CLI key name release, as APPLICATION.md states. The Grow publisher's CLI key name is not recorded in the source I read. Its runbook says the publisher custody is separate from operator and simulator custody and absent from the head (runbook.md:30–31). Hugh must identify the Grow key name for your signing step. I opened no key file and make no claim that the Lending key exists in a particular private config directory.\n\n6. With a valid new signed Grow packet and matching new TRUSTED values, growArchivePolicies admits every definition in that packet. It builds the registry from the semantic entry and other declared definitions, then adds ARCHIVE_DEVICE_APPLICATION_COHORT pins (apps/room/archive-policy.ts:24–49). Updating GROW_ARCHIVE_DEFINITIONS and the trusted packet together therefore admits the new Site and Room definition digests. The new base native Directory, Rules and Destination pins still differ from that old native cohort unless a separate reviewed change adds them.\n\nUntil AE-1, the release mismatch in item 4 prevents that registry from being constructed in the Grow Worker. A new application's alarms run the ordinary owners and skip archive through this.#archive?.run() (packages/scope/src/object.ts:980–989). They have no archive retry loop. Retired objects still return at alarm line 963. If a later change matches the configuration release while leaving the old directory, an admitted new application can reach target discovery: unsupported base birth policy or the configured-directory guard can yield no target (archive.ts:52–80). That path returns retry/unavailable and keeps its retry wake, as answered in db3adddb (archive-driver.ts:193–212; object.ts:989). AE-1 must therefore review both the directory binding and native policy coverage before enabling the new release's archive.\n\nI read docs/testing.md and the technical-writing skill. I changed no source, opened no key file, and ran no tests, compiler, build, install, deployment or signing command.","body":{"to":"planner"}}